        ["When should exercise sessions be scheduled for a person with Parkinson's disease who takes carbidopa-levodopa?",["During the medication 'on' period after a dose takes effect","Just before the next dose, when medication is wearing off","At random times to test unmedicated performance","Only in the late evening after all daily doses"],"Patients move best during 'on' periods. Training when medication is wearing off increases freezing and fall risk."],
        ["A patient with MS reports that her legs grow weaker and her vision blurs after a hot shower or exercising in a warm room. What is the MOST appropriate modification?",["Exercise in a cool environment and use cooling strategies","Exercise in a warm pool to relax spasticity","Stop exercising permanently to avoid relapse","Increase intensity to build heat tolerance"],"This is Uhthoff's phenomenon: elevated core temperature transiently worsens conduction in demyelinated nerves. Cooling strategies allow safe exercise."],
        ["A patient with relapsing-remitting MS reports overwhelming fatigue by mid-afternoon. Which recommendation is MOST appropriate?",["Schedule exercise earlier in the day with planned rest breaks","Exercise only in the evening when fatigue is highest","Perform long continuous sessions without rest to build endurance","Avoid all physical activity until the fatigue is gone"],"MS fatigue is managed with energy conservation: scheduling activity when energy is best, pacing, and rest breaks, while still exercising."],
        ["A patient presents with hyperreflexia and spasticity in the legs, along with atrophy and fasciculations in the hands. Sensation is intact. Which diagnosis BEST fits?",["Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Guillain-Barré syndrome","Multiple sclerosis","Myasthenia gravis"],"Mixed upper and lower motor neuron signs with spared sensation are characteristic of ALS. GBS is LMN only with areflexia."],
        ["Which exercise approach is MOST appropriate for a patient with early ALS?",["Moderate, submaximal exercise that avoids fatigue","High-intensity eccentric training to failure","Maximal strengthening of already weak muscles","Complete rest to preserve motor neurons"],"In ALS, overworking weak, denervated muscle can cause overwork damage. Moderate, non-fatiguing activity maintains function and ROM."],
        ["Two weeks after a GI illness, a patient develops symmetric weakness that started in the feet and is ascending, with absent reflexes. Which measure should the PT monitor MOST closely?",["Vital capacity","Deep tendon reflex return","Grip strength","Pain level"],"This presentation suggests Guillain-Barré syndrome, which can ascend to the respiratory muscles. Falling vital capacity can signal the need for ventilatory support."],
        ["A patient recovering from GBS feels much weaker the day after an intensive strengthening session. What is the BEST response?",["Reduce exercise intensity and avoid fatigue to prevent overwork weakness","Increase resistance because soreness indicates progress","Switch to maximal eccentric exercise to speed recovery","Discontinue all exercise until full strength returns"],"Recovering peripheral nerves are vulnerable to overwork weakness. Low-to-moderate, non-fatiguing exercise is appropriate."],
        ["About an hour after taking carbidopa-levodopa, a patient with Parkinson's disease shows involuntary writhing movements of the trunk and limbs. What is this MOST likely to be?",["Levodopa-induced dyskinesia","Resting tremor of Parkinson's","Essential tremor","Cerebellar intention tremor"],"Peak-dose dyskinesia is a choreiform side effect of long-term levodopa. Resting tremor is rhythmic and decreases with movement."],
        ["A patient with MS (EDSS 3.0) asks whether resistance training could trigger a relapse. What is the BEST response?",["Moderate exercise is safe and has not been shown to raise relapse risk","Exercise should be avoided because it often triggers new relapses","Only passive stretching is safe until the disease fully stabilizes","Resistance training is safe only in a heated pool environment"],"Exercise in MS improves fitness, fatigue, and walking without increasing relapse rates. Heat should be avoided, not sought."],
        ["A patient with ALS reports morning headaches, daytime sleepiness, and needing to sleep propped up on pillows. What is the MOST likely cause?",["Respiratory muscle weakness with nocturnal hypoventilation","Spasticity of the trunk and hip flexors interrupting sleep","Depression causing poor sleep quality and fatigue","Orthostatic hypotension from new medications"],"These are classic signs of nocturnal hypoventilation from diaphragm weakness. The PT should report them so respiratory support can be evaluated."],
        ["A patient recovering from Guillain-Barré syndrome asks how strength will return. Which statement is MOST accurate?",["Most recover well, often proximal to distal over months","Recovery is rare, and weakness usually keeps progressing","Strength returns within days once the fever resolves","Distal muscles recover first, then proximal ones last"],"Most people with GBS recover substantially over months, typically in a descending, proximal-to-distal pattern. Ongoing progression is not the usual course."],
        ["A patient with Parkinson's disease has bilateral symptoms and impaired balance on the pull test but remains physically independent. Which Hoehn and Yahr stage BEST fits?",["Stage 3","Stage 1","Stage 2","Stage 5"],"Stage 3 is bilateral disease with postural instability while still physically independent. Stage 2 is bilateral without balance impairment."]
